  In rapidity space, evolution is APPROXIMATELY UNIFORM!
  The events are spread roughly evenly from rapidity 2 to 11.
  This is because evolution generates novelty at a roughly
  EXPONENTIAL rate (constant progress per log-time), and
  rapidity = log/2 linearizes exponential growth.

======================================================================
5. ALLOMETRIC SCALING AND RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  Kleiber's law: metabolic rate B ~ M^{3/4} (M = body mass)
  In rapidity: rapidity(B) = (3/4)*rapidity(M) + const
  This is a LINEAR relationship in rapidity space!

  The 3/4 exponent means: for every 4 octaves of mass,
  metabolic rate increases by 3 octaves.

  Heart rate ~ M^{-1/4}: rapidity(HR) = -(1/4)*rapidity(M) + const
  Lifespan ~ M^{1/4}: rapidity(L) = (1/4)*rapidity(M) + const

  Total heartbeats in lifetime ~ HR * L ~ M^0 = CONSTANT!
  rapidity(total_beats) = rapidity(HR) + rapidity(L) = 0 + const
  About 1.5 billion heartbeats for all mammals.

  Animal             Mass(kg)  HR(bpm) Life(yr)    rap(M)  rap(HR)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------
  Shrew                   0.0     1000        2   -3.1073   3.4539
  Mouse                   0.0      600        3   -1.9560   3.1985
  Rat                     0.3      400        4   -0.6020   2.9957
  Rabbit                  2.0      200        9    0.3466   2.6492
  Cat                     4.0      150       15    0.6931   2.5053
  Dog                    20.0      100       13    1.4979   2.3026
  Human                  70.0       70       80    2.1242   2.1242
  Horse                 500.0       40       30    3.1073   1.8444
  Elephant             5000.0       30       70    4.2586   1.7006
  Blue whale         150000.0       10       80    5.9592   1.1513

  Verify Kleiber: slope of rapidity(HR) vs rapidity(M) should be -1/4.
  Linear regression: slope = -0.2571 (expected -0.25)
  Intercept = 2.7094

======================================================================
6. FIBONACCI IN BIOLOGY AS RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  Fibonacci numbers appear in phyllotaxis (leaf arrangement),
  petal counts, spiral counts, etc.
  F_n ~ phi^n/sqrt(5).
  rapidity(F_n) ~ n*ln(phi)/2 = n * 0.2406

  n    F_n    rapidity(F_n)   n*ln(phi)/2
   1       1      0.0000          0.2406
   2       2      0.3466          0.4812
   3       3      0.5493          0.7218
   4       5      0.8047          0.9624
   5       8      1.0397          1.2030
   6      13      1.2825          1.4436
   7      21      1.5223          1.6842
   8      34      1.7632          1.9248
   9      55      2.0037          2.1655
  10      89      2.2443          2.4061
  11     144      2.4849          2.6467
  12     233      2.7255          2.8873
  13     377      2.9661          3.1279

  Fibonacci rapidity grows LINEARLY in n with slope ln(phi)/2 = 0.2406.
  This is the rapidity of the golden ratio divided by 2.
  Each Fibonacci step adds 0.24 rapidity units ~ 0.7 octaves.

======================================================================
7. DNA INFORMATION CONTENT IN RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  DNA uses 4 bases (A, C, G, T).
  Information per base = log2(4) = 2 bits.
  A genome of N bases contains 2N bits = N log2(4) bits.

  The number of possible sequences of length N: 4^N.
  rapidity(4^N) = N*ln(4)/2 = N*ln(2) = N * 0.6931
  Each base adds ln(2) = one OCTAVE of rapidity to the sequence space!

  For the human genome (N = 3.2 billion):
  rapidity of sequence space = 2.2181e+09
  = 3e+09 octaves!

  But actual information content is much less (most DNA is non-coding).
  Functional human genome ~ 1.5% coding => ~ 48 million coding bases.
  Functional sequence space rapidity = 3.3271e+07
  = 5e+07 octaves of functional rapidity

======================================================================
8. DOUBLING TIMES AND RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  Cell division time = doubling time.
  After n doublings: 2^n cells.
  rapidity(2^n) = n*ln(2)/2 = n * 0.3466
  Each division adds exactly one OCTAVE of rapidity.

  From 1 cell to a human (~37 trillion cells):
  Divisions needed: log2(37e12) = 45.1
  Rapidity: 15.6210
  = 45 octaves from zygote to adult

  From 1 bacterium to E. coli colony of 10^9:
  Divisions: log2(10^9) = 29.9
  = 30 octaves of bacterial growth

======================================================================
9. EXTINCTION RATES AND RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  Background extinction rate: ~0.1 species per million species-years
  Current rate: ~100-1000x background

  Mass extinction events (% species lost):
  Event                  % lost   Mya    survival  rap(survival)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------
  End Ordovician            85%   444        0.15        -0.8673
  Late Devonian             75%   372        0.25        -0.5493
  End Permian               96%   252        0.04        -1.5890
  End Triassic              80%   201        0.20        -0.6931
  End Cretaceous            76%    66        0.24        -0.5763
  Current (est.)            30%     0        0.70         0.4236

  The End Permian (96% loss) has survival rapidity -1.5890
  This is DEEPLY NEGATIVE — near total extinction in rapidity terms.

